{
 "about": "The in-scope capability-manifest rows dart_monty_core's M3 owns, each with the backends it must be PRESENT on (tool/parity/score.py --target; shown, not asserted, until --assert-target lands at the cut). Every in-scope manifest row is in `rows` or in `excluded` with the clause that excludes it; `judgement` ids point at the rows the rule did not decide mechanically (the owner-reviewed list). An `allowed` entry names why a target row may be non-PRESENT; it is valid only as the approved rule or an owner-approved exception says, and is stale (fails) once its row is PRESENT on those backends.",
 "clauses": {
  "A1": {
   "text": "area filesystem: mounts and the memory filesystem are M6 (C0a)",
   "owner": "M6"
  },
  "A2": {
   "text": "area telemetry",
   "owner": "M6"
  },
  "A3": {
   "text": "area deployment: prebuilt workers and delivery",
   "owner": "M4"
  },
  "A4": {
   "text": "area runtime_cli: the upstream command line",
   "owner": "M4"
  },
  "B1": {
   "text": "os_calls row whose every parity case needs a mounted filesystem (rows.json case sets `fs`)",
   "owner": "M6"
  },
  "C1": {
   "text": "upstream lacks the row in its bindings: neither python_cite nor js_cite in the manifest, and no parity case, probe, worker set or session rule compares it against the python binding (a Rust-API oracle does not count)",
   "owner": "upstream"
  },
  "G7": {
   "text": "browser aggregate-memory resource_limits rows (M6, G7): the web backends are not targets for the row, the native backend is",
   "owner": "M6"
  },
  "D1": {
   "text": "standalone type checking: the rule makes only type_checking in the session a target; M6 removes standalone typeCheck",
   "owner": "M6"
  },
  "D2": {
   "text": "upstream has no behaviour to compare: the C1 gate records it as core-gated (c1-gate.json core_gated), upstream's sinks and cancellation are its binding's own",
   "owner": "upstream"
  }
 },
